      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm good with that for now. Are there any neighborhoods or spots in New York City that have a Spanish vibe or are known for Spanish culture?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some places to visit in New York City that are closer to the Spanish culture?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "If you're looking for Spanish culture in NYC, Mercado Little Spain at Hudson Yards is a standout destination\u2014a food hall created by Chef Jos\u00e9 Andr\u00e9s, offering authentic Spanish cuisine, groceries, and dining experiences reminiscent of Spain [clueweb22-en0014-64-05304:2]. Historically, 'Little Spain' on 14th Street in Manhattan was a hub for Spanish immigrants and culture, as highlighted in the documentary 'Little Spain' [clueweb22-en0011-89-10290:4]. While the area is less prominent today, its legacy remains part of the city's cultural fabric. Additionally, Spanish Harlem (El Barrio) around Lexington and 116th Street is known for its Spanish-speaking community, cultural identity, and local businesses with Spanish roots [clueweb22-en0038-40-10043:6]. These neighborhoods and spots offer a taste of Spanish heritage, food, and community in New York City. Would you like recommendations for Spanish restaurants or tapas bars in NYC?",
