      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yes, actually! I\u2019d rather visit quieter museums than crowded ones. Are there any hidden gems or less-known museums in NYC you\u2019d recommend?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is so special about New York's subway system?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "If you prefer quieter museums, consider BLDG 92 in Fort Greene, Brooklyn, which highlights the history of the Brooklyn Navy Yard and features exhibits on shipbuilding and the contributions of women and people of color during WWII [DOC-1]. The Panorama of the City of New York, located in Queens, is a fascinating, detailed scale model of the entire city and is often less crowded than major art museums [DOC-1]. The New York City Fire Museum in Hudson Square offers an in-depth look at the history of the city's fire department and is a hidden gem for history enthusiasts [DOC-4]. These museums provide a more peaceful and unique experience compared to the larger, busier institutions. Would you like directions or ticket information for any of these museums?",
          "citations": {
