      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great recommendations! Any advice on how to navigate the subway effectively?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is so special about New York's subway system?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "To navigate the New York subway smoothly\u2014especially as a visitor from Spain who prefers cash for small purchases\u2014here\u2019s what matters most:\n\n**Payment & Tickets**\n- Buy a MetroCard at any subway station (vending machines accept cash, cards, and bills). There\u2019s a $1 fee for a new card.\n- For stays of several days, the unlimited weekly MetroCard ($33 + $1 card fee) is the best value.\n- Alternatively, OMNY (contactless tap with card/phone) is being phased in, but MetroCard is still the main option for unlimited rides and cash payments.\n\n**Using the Subway**\n- The subway runs 24/7 and covers nearly all of NYC. Fares are flat ($2.75 per ride), no zones.\n- Stations are clearly marked; maps are posted in stations and cars\u2014grab a paper map or download one before your trip.\n- Lines are identified by numbers/letters and colors. Major hubs (e.g., Grand Central, Penn Station) make transfers easy.\n- Avoid taxis/Uber for most trips\u2014subway",
