          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I really appreciate that info! What are some Spanish cultural spots or restaurants I shouldn't miss in New York?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some places to visit in New York City that are closer to the Spanish culture?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For authentic Spanish culture and cuisine in New York, El Quijote in Chelsea is the standout choice:\n\n**El Quijote (226 W 23rd St, Chelsea)**\n- NYC\u2019s oldest Spanish restaurant, recently renovated but retaining its classic, kitschy Spanish decor\u2014think Don Quixote murals and statues.\n- Menu highlights: Generous portions of traditional Spanish dishes\u2014paella, chorizo, garlic shrimp, and sangria. Seafood is a specialty.\n- Atmosphere: Historic, lively but not overwhelmingly crowded; a good fit if you prefer a more relaxed, sit-down experience over noisy tapas bars.\n- Cash is accepted for small purchases, but main meals are typically paid by card\u2014bring both to be safe.\n- Staff are experienced with dietary needs, but always double-check for peanut ingredients due to your allergy.\n\nIf you\u2019re interested in broader Spanish culture (art, events), let me know if you want recommendations for galleries or cultural centers with Spanish ties. Would you like directions to El Quijote or suggestions for quieter Spanish cafes?",
