      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That sounds pretty practical! Are there any areas or neighborhoods in NYC that feel particularly Spanish or have strong Spanish influences?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some places to visit in New York City that are closer to the Spanish culture?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes\u2014NYC has a few spots with distinctly Spanish roots and broader Hispanic influences:  - Little Spain (West 14th St, between 7th\u20138th Aves, Chelsea): Historically called Calle Catorce/\u201cEspa\u00f1a Chica.\u201d Walk the block for remnants of the enclave. Key stops: Spanish Benevolent Society (La Nacional), 239 W 14th St, and the Church of Our Lady of Guadalupe, 229 W 14th St. Easy to combine with a Chelsea/Greenwich Village stroll.  - Washington Heights: The Hispanic Society of America (Broadway at 155th St) holds exceptional art from Spain and Portugal (and some Latin America). A quieter, scholarly-feeling visit.  - Midtown: Instituto Cervantes (near Midtown) offers exhibits, talks, and the city\u2019s largest Spanish-language library\u2014great for a quick cultural fix or book browsing.  - Spanish Harlem (El Barrio, East Harlem): Strong Puerto Rican/Latino heritage. Visit El Museo del Barrio on Museum Mile for Latin American and Caribbean art with deep NYC ties.  Tip: Check each venue\u2019s current hours/exhibit schedule before you go.",
          "citations": {
