          "user_rubrik": "What are some places to visit in New York City that are closer to the Spanish culture?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 NYC has a lively Spanish cultural presence you\u2019ll enjoy, especially as a history teacher and rare-book collector:  - Hispanic Society of America (Washington Heights): calm, encyclopedic collections of Spanish art, literature, and manuscripts \u2014 ideal for quiet study and rare-book interest.   - Instituto Cervantes (Midtown/Upper West events): talks, film series, language & cultural events showcasing Spain.   - Museo del Barrio & Spanish Harlem (El Barrio): Puerto Rican/Latinx focus but lots of Iberian-influenced music, dance, galleries and community events.   - Major museums (The Met, The Frick, MoMA): strong holdings and rotating exhibits of Vel\u00e1zquez, Goya, El Greco and Spanish Golden Age works \u2014 book timed weekday visits to avoid crowds.   - Flamenco and music: annual flamenco/Spanish music performances (check Flamenco Festival NYC and venue calendars like Symphony Space).   - Book hunting: Strand Bookstore, specialist antiquarian shops and auction houses \u2014 great for rare finds with Spanish-language material.  Want me to map these by subway lines from your Manhattan stay and suggest quiet visiting times?",
