          "text": "great \u2014 here are practical, nyc \u2011 specific spots and training tips tailored to your fitness level and routine : places to run - central park \u2014 many quieter corners ; the full loop is close to 10 km if you want a long \u2011 run simulation. try weekday mornings. - central park reservoir \u2014 good for controlled laps and pacing. - hudson river greenway / riverside park \u2014 flat, long, very peaceful for tempo runs. - icahn stadium ( randall \u2019 s island ) \u2014 public 400m track for interval work. - prospect park ( brooklyn ) \u2014 if you want quieter, leafy long runs away from manhattan. - join nyrr or local running \u2011 store group runs for guided, beginner \u2011 friendly sessions. training tips - build long runs gradually ( e. g., 6 km \u2192 up to 12 km as race nears ). - include cross \u2011 training ( bike, swim, walking ) and bodyweight strength ( push \u2011 ups, chin \u2011 ups, light weights ). - use \u201c microdosing \u201d : 3 \u00d7 20 \u2011 minute easy sessions after training across the week for volume without long extra sessions. - schedule downtime ( lunchtime nap ) and pick weekday mornings to avoid crowds. - pack peanut \u2011 free snacks and",
